  <p class="lead">
    Few plants on Earth carry as much living history as the <strong>Ginkgo Biloba</strong> — a tree species that has survived more than 200 million years and is sometimes called a "living fossil." Today, its leaves are one of the most studied herbal remedies in the world, valued especially for supporting <strong>memory, mental clarity, and healthy blood circulation</strong>. This guide explains what Ginkgo Biloba is, what the evidence shows, how it is traditionally used, and how to take it safely.
  </p>

  <div class="takeaways" role="region" aria-label="Key takeaways">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ginkgo Biloba is one of the oldest tree species on Earth — used in traditional Chinese medicine for over 1,000 years.</li>
      <li>The active compounds — <strong>flavonoids</strong> and <strong>terpene lactones</strong> — are antioxidants that may support healthy blood flow to the brain and limbs.</li>
      <li>It is most often used to support <strong>memory, focus, mental clarity, peripheral circulation, and antioxidant defence</strong>.</li>
      <li>Typical adult dose: <strong>120–240 mg/day</strong> of standardised leaf extract, taken consistently for 4–6 weeks before judging effect.</li>
      <li>Avoid Ginkgo if you take blood thinners, are scheduled for surgery, are pregnant or breastfeeding, or have a bleeding disorder.</li>
      <li>Dynapharm offers Ginkgo in <strong>Yeeginkgo Tablets</strong>, <strong>Ginali Capsules</strong>, and <strong>Ginkgo Ginseng Coffee</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
