  <h2 id="what-is-ginkgo">What is Ginkgo Biloba?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ginkgo Biloba</strong> (sometimes spelled <em>Gingko</em>) is a deciduous tree native to China, recognisable by its distinctive fan-shaped leaves that turn brilliant gold in autumn. It is the only living member of an ancient plant family — every other relative has been extinct for tens of millions of years. Some Ginkgo trees on temple grounds in Asia are estimated to be more than 1,500 years old.
  </p>
  <p>
    In modern wellness, the term "Ginkgo Biloba" almost always refers to a <strong>standardised extract of the dried leaves</strong> — typically labelled as containing 24% flavone glycosides and 6% terpene lactones (often abbreviated EGb 761 in research). This is the form found in the vast majority of clinical studies and reputable supplements.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <Image src={inlineImg1} alt="Bright green Ginkgo Biloba leaves with their characteristic fan shape, photographed against natural foliage" widths={[640, 1024, 1280]} sizes="(min-width: 768px) 720px, 100vw" loading="lazy" />
