  <h2 id="history">A short history: from ancient Asia to modern medicine</h2>
  <p>
    Ginkgo Biloba has been used in <strong>traditional Chinese medicine for more than 1,000 years</strong> — historical texts mention both the leaves and the seeds for supporting respiratory health, circulation, and longevity. The tree was introduced to Europe in the 18th century, and to North America soon after, but its rise as a mainstream herbal supplement only began in the 1960s and 1970s, when European researchers — particularly in Germany — began standardising leaf extracts and testing them in clinical trials for memory and circulatory health.
  </p>
  <p>
    Today, Ginkgo Biloba is one of the <strong>most widely sold herbal products</strong> in Europe and is registered as a traditional herbal medicine in several countries.
  </p>

  <h2 id="active-compounds">What is in Ginkgo Biloba? The active compounds</h2>
  <p>
    Ginkgo's effects are attributed to two main groups of natural compounds, both concentrated in the leaves:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flavonoids (flavone glycosides)</strong> — plant antioxidants that help neutralise free radicals and may protect the lining of blood vessels.</li>
    <li><strong>Terpene lactones (ginkgolides and bilobalide)</strong> — unique to Ginkgo; thought to support healthy blood flow and protect nerve cells from oxidative stress.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Standardised extracts are designed so that every dose contains a <strong>consistent percentage</strong> of these active compounds, which is why standardisation matters for both safety and efficacy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="benefits">Health benefits of Ginkgo Biloba</h2>
  <p>
    Ginkgo Biloba has been studied for a wide range of uses. Below are the benefits with the strongest combination of <strong>traditional use</strong> and <strong>modern research</strong>.
  </p>

  <h3>1. May support memory, focus and cognitive function</h3>
  <p>
    The most common reason people take Ginkgo Biloba is to support memory and mental clarity. Several clinical trials in older adults with mild memory complaints have reported small but meaningful improvements in attention, recall, and processing speed after 8–24 weeks of daily standardised extract. Results in healthy younger adults are more mixed.
  </p>

  <h3>2. May promote healthy blood circulation</h3>
  <p>
    Ginkgo's terpene lactones are believed to support healthy blood flow, including <strong>microcirculation</strong> in the brain and limbs. Traditionally, this is why Ginkgo is associated with relief from cold hands and feet, and with cognitive sharpness — the brain depends on a steady supply of oxygen-rich blood.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Powerful antioxidant activity</h3>
  <p>
    The flavonoid fraction of Ginkgo is a potent antioxidant. By helping neutralise free radicals, Ginkgo may contribute to <strong>cellular protection</strong>, especially in tissues that are highly active metabolically — including the brain, heart, and eyes.
  </p>

  <h3>4. May ease tinnitus and dizziness</h3>
  <p>
    Some studies, particularly in older European literature, suggest Ginkgo may help reduce the severity of tinnitus and certain types of dizziness, especially when caused by reduced inner-ear circulation. Evidence is mixed and benefits are typically modest, but some people find it useful.
  </p>

  <h3>5. May support eye health</h3>
  <p>
    Because of its antioxidant and circulation-supporting effects, Ginkgo has been studied for age-related macular health and glaucoma support. Research is preliminary, and Ginkgo should not replace prescribed treatment for any eye condition.
  </p>

  <h3>6. May help with stress and mood balance</h3>
  <p>
    A small body of research suggests Ginkgo may help the body manage day-to-day mental stress. It is sometimes paired with adaptogenic herbs like <strong>Ginseng</strong> for combined cognitive and stress-resilience benefits.
  </p>

