  <h2 id="how-to-use">How to use Ginkgo Biloba</h2>
  <h3>Recommended daily dose</h3>
  <p>
    For standardised leaf extract (24% flavone glycosides / 6% terpene lactones), most clinical studies and traditional medicine references suggest <strong>120 mg to 240 mg per day</strong> for adults, usually split into two or three doses with meals.
  </p>
  <p>
    Always follow the directions on the product you are taking. <strong>Consistency is more important than dose</strong> — most benefits emerge after 4 to 12 weeks of daily use.
  </p>
  <h3>How to choose a good Ginkgo product</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Look for <strong>standardised leaf extract</strong>, not raw leaf powder.</li>
    <li>Check for the typical 24/6 standardisation (flavone glycosides / terpene lactones).</li>
    <li>Choose a manufacturer with documented quality controls.</li>
    <li>Pick a format that suits your routine — tablet, capsule or beverage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="safety">Safety, side effects and interactions</h2>
  <p>
    Standardised Ginkgo extract is generally well tolerated by healthy adults. Mild side effects, when they occur, may include headache, mild stomach upset, or dizziness — these typically resolve when the dose is reduced or the supplement is stopped.
  </p>
  <h3>When to avoid Ginkgo Biloba</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>If you take <strong>blood-thinning medication</strong> (warfarin, aspirin, clopidogrel) — Ginkgo may increase bleeding risk.</li>
    <li>If you have a <strong>bleeding disorder</strong> or are scheduled for surgery within 2 weeks.</li>
    <li>If you are <strong>pregnant or breastfeeding</strong> — safety has not been established.</li>
    <li>If you have <strong>epilepsy or a history of seizures</strong> — Ginkgo may lower seizure threshold in rare cases.</li>
    <li>If you are <strong>allergic</strong> to Ginkgo or related plants.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Never consume raw Ginkgo seeds</strong> — they contain a natural toxin (ginkgotoxin) that can be dangerous in significant amounts. Standardised leaf extracts are processed to remove or minimise this compound.
  </p>

